EKS architecture

Suppose you have an application running in EKS:

                AWS
                 |
              EKS Cluster
                 |
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       |         |         |
    VPC CNI   CoreDNS   EBS CSI Driver
       |         |         |
   Networking    DNS     Storage
       |         |         |
    Pod IPs    Service    EBS Volume
1. VPC CNI — Networking

VPC CNI gives IP addresses to Kubernetes Pods from the AWS VPC.

Example:

Node
10.0.1.10

Application Pod
10.0.1.25

MySQL Pod
10.0.1.30

The Pod gets an IP from the AWS VPC subnet.

So:

Application Pod
      |
      | network
      ↓
MySQL Pod

VPC CNI is responsible for making this Pod networking work.

Interview answer:

AWS VPC CNI is the Kubernetes networking plugin used in EKS. It assigns VPC IP addresses to Pods and allows Pods to communicate using the AWS VPC network.

2. CoreDNS — DNS

CoreDNS helps Pods find other Kubernetes services by name instead of IP address.

For example:

Application Pod
      |
      | mysql-service
      ↓
MySQL Service
      |
      ↓
MySQL Pod

The application doesn't need to remember:

10.0.1.30

It can use:

mysql-service

CoreDNS converts:

mysql-service
      ↓
Service IP
      ↓
MySQL Pod

Interview answer:

CoreDNS provides DNS-based service discovery inside the Kubernetes cluster.

3. EBS CSI Driver — Storage

Suppose MySQL needs permanent storage.

MySQL Pod
    |
    | PersistentVolumeClaim
    ↓
EBS CSI Driver
    |
    ↓
AWS EBS Volume

The EBS CSI Driver allows Kubernetes to create and attach AWS EBS volumes to Pods.

For example:

MySQL Pod
   |
   ↓
PVC
   |
   ↓
PV
   |
   ↓
EBS Volume

If the MySQL Pod is deleted:

Old MySQL Pod ❌
       |
       | data remains
       ↓
   EBS Volume
       |
       ↓
New MySQL Pod

The new Pod can mount the same persistent storage, depending on the PVC/PV configuration.

Interview answer:

The AWS EBS CSI Driver allows Kubernetes to provision and attach Amazon EBS volumes for persistent Pod storage.

