In one line:

VPC CNI = networking, CoreDNS = DNS/service discovery, EBS CSI = storage.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. CNI = Networking

CNI (Container Network Interface) is responsible for giving a Pod network connectivity.

In EKS, AWS VPC CNI gives the Pod an IP address from the VPC.

Node
10.0.1.10
   |
   ├── App Pod
   |    10.0.1.20
   |
   └── MySQL Pod
        10.0.1.30

So CNI handles:

Pod → Pod
Pod → Service/network
Pod → Internet/AWS resources

Think:

CNI = "How does the Pod communicate?"

2. CoreDNS = Name Resolution

CoreDNS allows you to use a name instead of an IP address.

Suppose MySQL has a Service:

mysql-service
      |
      ↓
10.96.20.10
      |
      ↓
MySQL Pod

Your application can simply say:

mysql-service

CoreDNS resolves:

mysql-service
      ↓
10.96.20.10

Then Kubernetes Service sends the traffic to the MySQL Pod.

Think:

CoreDNS = "What IP/address does this name refer to?"

How they work together
Application Pod
      |
      | 1. "I want mysql-service"
      ↓
   CoreDNS
      |
      | 2. "mysql-service = 10.96.20.10"
      ↓
Application Pod
      |
      | 3. Send network traffic
      ↓
    CNI
      |
      ↓
 Kubernetes Service
      |
      ↓
  MySQL Pod

So remember:

CNI     → gives networking/IP connectivity
CoreDNS → resolves names to IP addresses

Interview answer:

CNI is responsible for Pod networking and connectivity, while CoreDNS provides DNS-based service discovery so Pods can communicate with Services using names instead of IP addresses.





